WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.980
<v Instructor>So question nine now.</v>

2
00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.810
The company has built an online ticketing

3
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.210
web application on AWS.

4
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.660
The application runs as containers on Amazon ECS

5
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.520
with the AWS Fargate launch type

6
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.320
behind an Application Load Balancer

7
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:18.150
and uses images that are stored in Amazon ECR repository.

8
00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.350
The application will store data

9
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.110
in an Amazon Aurora MySQL DB cluster,

10
00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:26.100
and the company has set up a domain name in Amazon Route 53.

11
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.980
And now we want to deploy the application across two regions

12
00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.840
with an active-active configuration,

13
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.570
which means both of them will receive traffic.

14
00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.780
So we need to have a combination of steps to make this work

15
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.020
with the least amount of change to the architecture.

16
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:40.853
So let's have a look.

17
00:00:40.853 --> 00:00:42.690
So this is our current architecture right now.

18
00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.190
We have Route 53 that is directing traffic to our ALB.

19
00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.700
We have behind ALB the ECS Fargate service connected

20
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.770
to an Aurora MySQL database.

21
00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:56.970
The ECS Fargate service receives all the images from ECR.

22
00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.160
And so everything is in one region right now.

23
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.980
So for this question, we have actually three option groups.

24
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.550
So let's compare A to B, C to D, and E to F.

25
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.700
A is set up cross-region replication to the second region

26
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.320
for the ECR images.

27
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.700
So that means we have a second region

28
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.670
and ECR has a replication feature.

29
00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.610
Okay, this sounds plausible. What is the second option?

30
00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.280
Create a VPC endpoint from the ECR repository

31
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.690
in the second region.

32
00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.760
So here we create a VPC endpoint.

33
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.890
And one thing we should know is that

34
00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.160
if an option seems very plausible, the other one doesn't.

35
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.410
So the first one seems plausible,

36
00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:35.610
cross-region replication of ECR images is a feature of ECR,

37
00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.170
so it is the right answer.

38
00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.450
But on top of it, B is the wrong answer

39
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.720
because VPC endpoints do not work to get images

40
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.640
from ECR across regions.

41
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.580
Here, the reason why we have this cross-region replication

42
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.210
is that because the images need to be local

43
00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.490
to the region where they're getting pulled.

44
00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:57.063
So the correct answer here is answer A.

45
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.930
Then we have edit the ECS service configuration

46
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.410
by adding a second deployment target to the second region.

47
00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:07.920
So here we have one ECS Fargate service

48
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.980
managing two regions, a multi-region deploy.

49
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.240
And then the second one is

50
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.360
to deploy the ECS cluster, Fargate service,

51
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.160
and ALB to the second region

52
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.180
and set up Route 53 latency-based routing.

53
00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:25.110
So if there was a multi-region deploy for Fargate,

54
00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.190
this could be a possibility,

55
00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.380
but still it wouldn't be fully correct,

56
00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.690
but there is no such feature for Fargate.

57
00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.960
Here, as we've seen for any active-active type

58
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.790
of deployment, it is the best thing

59
00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.750
to redeploy the entire solution into another region

60
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:44.400
and use indeed a DNS feature of Route 53

61
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.820
to redirect to the region that is the closest to the user,

62
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.740
for example, based on latency.

63
00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.740
So here redeploying the architecture is the correct choice.

64
00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.870
This is answer D.

65
00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.730
And finally, change the database

66
00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.190
by using Amazon DynamoDB global tables

67
00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.110
in the two desired regions.

68
00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.640
So we have active-active replication

69
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.240
and we know that DynamoDB is definitely

70
00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.130
has a global database feature

71
00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.620
with this active-active replication.

72
00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.540
Or to use an Aurora global database

73
00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.390
with write forwarding enabled in the second region.

74
00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:18.720
So we also know that Aurora is a global database.

75
00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.390
So both these answers could be correct,

76
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.300
but the question specifically asked how to do this

77
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.550
with the least amount of changes,

78
00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.230
and we're already using Aurora

79
00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.170
and this is a feature of Aurora to be able to be replicated

80
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.300
and active across regions.

81
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.340
So therefore the correct answer here is going

82
00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:36.840
to be answer F.

83
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.060
So here is our final architecture

84
00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.280
and this is a classic for AWS.

85
00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.720
So we have one region

86
00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.120
with a web application has been deployed,

87
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.390
a second region with this exact same deployment,

88
00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.580
and we enable cross-region replication

89
00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.680
for whatever is needed to be replicated

90
00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.770
such as the ECR registry and images,

91
00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.110
and also make sure that we have a database

92
00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.410
that is replicated and also accepts the writes.

93
00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.660
At least here the write requests are forwarded

94
00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.790
to a main Aurora database.

95
00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:08.700
And how to distribute the load between these two regions?

96
00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.210
Well for this, we use Route 53 with a record name,

97
00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.850
with latency endpoint, which is going to redirect us

98
00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.800
to the closest ALB.

99
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.720
So that's it for this lecture, I hope you liked it,

100
00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.670
and I will see you in the next lecture.

